  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 243 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nThursday and into Friday will remain warm as the region remains \nunder the influence of a ridge that will continue to slide to the \neast. By the weekend, that same ridge will have weakened and \ntemperatures will begin to \"cool off\" by about 2-4 degrees areawide. \nThis will be the difference for most between reaching near 95F \nrather than near 100F+ that has been seen the last several days. \nAdditionally, this will increase rain chances, mainly over the \nhigher terrain and Big Bend. With no synoptic scale subsistence to \nsuppress showers and storms, a more summer-like, monsoon pattern \nreturns with isolated to scattered showers and storms recurring most \nafternoons. Though we are now technically in climatological fall, \nthere does not look to be a significant cool down in the extended \nforecast. \n\n-Stickney",
